#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# hypergeometric_0f1

#% text
# `hypergeometric_0f1(b, z)` gives the confluent hypergeometric limit function `0F1(; b; z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The series is the sum of `z^k/((b)_k*k!)` (DLMF 16.2.1), an entire function of `z`. Bessel functions are special cases.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values. `hypergeometric_0f1` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
hypergeometric_0f1(1, 1.0)
#% output
#= 2.2795853023360673

#% cell
hypergeometric_0f1(2.5, -3.0)
#% output
#= 0.21423710771131335

#% cell
n(hypergeometric_0f1(1/2, 1), 30)
#% output
#= 3.7621956910836314595622134777737461

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
hypergeometric_0f1(1.0, 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.7517341827138083 + 0.9722916273066612im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `0F1(; 1; -x^2/4)` is `bessel_j(0, x)`:

#% cell
hypergeometric_0f1(1, -1.0) - bessel_j(0, 2.0)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% text
# `0F1(; 1/2; x^2/4)` is `cosh(x)`:

#% cell
hypergeometric_0f1(0.5, 1.0) - cosh(2.0)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `hypergeometric_1f1`, `bessel_j`, `bessel_i`, `hypergeometric_pfq`
